      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="formulation-design">
        <h2>Rinse-Off vs Leave-In Conditioning: Formulation Design Principles</h2>

        <p>
          The distinction between rinse-off and leave-in conditioner formulations runs deeper than product texture or use instruction — it reflects fundamentally different deposition objectives, active level requirements, and compatibility constraints. A rinse-off conditioner is applied to saturated wet hair, allowed a brief contact time (typically 30–120 seconds), and then flushed with water. The formulation must therefore achieve maximum cationic deposition during a very brief exposure window, which demands high-molecular-weight, long-chain cationic actives with the thermodynamic drive to adsorb rapidly and tenaciously onto the cuticle before the dilution wave of rinsing arrives.
        </p>

        <p>
          Leave-in conditioners operate under opposite constraints. They remain on the hair until the next wash, which imposes requirements for low residue, no build-up with repeated use, and compatibility with subsequent styling products. Cationic polymers (Polyquaternium-10, Polyquaternium-11, Polyquaternium-44) are the conditioning actives of choice in leave-in formulations: they deposit a thin, lightweight film with negligible sensory weight, and their high molecular weight means they are too large to penetrate the cuticle and cause protein–polymer interaction issues. The pH of leave-in conditioners is typically adjusted upward slightly (pH 4.5–5.5) compared to rinse-off formulations, because the hair is exposed to the leave-in product for extended periods and cumulative acid exposure from very low pH products can affect colour stability in colour-treated hair.
        </p>

        <p>
          Formulation trends in the hair conditioning sector are driven by three concurrent pressures: the clean beauty movement away from silicones and traditional cationic surfactants, the growth of natural and organic claims, and the requirement to perform across increasingly diverse hair types (straight, wavy, curly, coily). Bio-based behentrimonium methosulfate derived from rapeseed or coconut feedstocks addresses the natural/organic claim without sacrificing the LGN-forming performance of petroleum-derived BTMS. Silicone-free systems using plant-derived emollients (baobab oil, argan oil, rice bran wax) and novel ester-based slip agents address the clean beauty demand, though these typically require higher cationic active levels to compensate for the loss of silicone lubricity on highly processed or chemically treated hair types.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What makes cationic surfactants effective in hair conditioners?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Hair is negatively charged at cosmetic use pH (approximately 3.5–5.5) because the keratin surface carries ionisable carboxylate and sulfonate groups. Cationic quaternary ammonium surfactants carry a permanent positive charge on their nitrogen atom, which drives strong electrostatic adsorption onto the negatively charged cuticle surface. This deposits a thin lubricating film that reduces the coefficient of friction between cuticle scales, lowering wet combing force, improving detangling, and imparting the smooth, soft feel of conditioned hair. Anionic surfactants (shampoo) carry a negative charge and cannot adsorb onto the hair — they remove conditioning films — which is why conditioning follows shampooing rather than occurring simultaneously.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is BTMS-50 and why is it widely used in conditioner formulation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">BTMS-50 is behentrimonium methosulfate combined with cetyl alcohol at 50% active. It is uniquely capable of forming a lamellar gel network (LGN) in water without a separate conventional emulsifier. When melted, combined with the water phase, and cooled, the behentrimonium methosulfate and cetyl alcohol co-crystallise into an ordered lamellar liquid crystal structure that produces the characteristic pearlescent, creamy texture of rinse-off conditioners. BTMS-50 delivers two functions simultaneously: cationic cuticle conditioning and product texture/stability through LGN formation. The methosulfate counterion is also considered milder than the chloride counterion used in BTAC, an advantage for scalp-contact formulations.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is a lamellar gel network in a hair conditioner?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">A lamellar gel network (LGN) forms when a cationic surfactant (typically BTMS) co-crystallises with fatty alcohols (cetearyl alcohol, cetyl alcohol) in water during cooling from the manufacturing melt temperature. The structure consists of alternating crystalline bilayers of fatty alcohol and cationic surfactant with aqueous channels between them — a lamellar liquid crystalline phase that traps water within its ordered layer structure. This creates the thick, pearlescent, creamy consistency of a rinse-off conditioner, provides physical stability against phase separation, and enables the thixotropic flow needed for application. Disruption of the LGN through incompatible actives, incorrect pH, or poor cooling rate is a primary cause of conditioner instability.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do rinse-off and leave-in conditioners differ in formulation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Rinse-off conditioners prioritise maximum deposition during brief wet contact time — they use high-substantivity cationic surfactants (BTAC or BTMS-50) and silicones for slip and shine, formulated at pH 3.5–4.5. Leave-in conditioners must be lightweight enough not to build up with repeated daily use: they favour cationic polymers (Polyquaternium-10, Polyquaternium-11) for lightweight film formation, use lower active levels, and are formulated at pH 4.5–5.5. Viscosity is significantly lower in leave-ins — from thin sprays to light creams — and heavy silicones are avoided or replaced with volatile options that evaporate after styling.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What cationic polymers are used in hair conditioners?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Polyquaternium-10 (cationic hydroxyethylcellulose) is the most widely used cationic polymer in rinse-off and leave-in conditioners — it deposits a lightweight, non-greasy film with good detangling and antistatic performance. Polyquaternium-11 (vinyl pyrrolidone / dimethylaminoethyl methacrylate copolymer) is used in styling conditioners for combined conditioning and light hold. Polyquaternium-7 (acrylamide/DADMAC copolymer) provides excellent wet slip with minimal residue. These polymers are particularly valuable in 2-in-1 shampoo-conditioner products because they deposit via coacervate formation during rinse-off dilution, even in the presence of the anionic surfactants in the shampoo base.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Are silicone-free hair conditioners as effective as those containing dimethicone?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Silicones — particularly dimethicone and amodimethicone — contribute exceptional lubrication, shine, and thermal protection that is difficult to replicate with cationic surfactants alone. Amodimethicone's amine groups adsorb selectively onto the most damaged cuticle regions, providing targeted repair. Silicone-free conditioners can achieve good detangling and softness using behentrimonium chloride or BTMS-50 with plant-derived emollients (shea butter, argan oil, plant esters) and cationic polymers, but typically deliver lower shine and inferior wet slip on highly damaged or bleached hair. For fine hair or consumers experiencing silicone build-up, silicone-free formulations offer the benefit of lighter feel and no cumulative residue.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

